# complex — `DREAM.md` §14.2, `scientific-libraries.md` §5.10 and
# `intrinsics-math-physics.md` §5.7: `use complex (Complex)`.
#
# The second module of the standard library written in Science, bundled the way
# `io` is (`science_resolve::stdlib`). It needs no runtime at all: every
# operation below is arithmetic on two `F64`s.
#
# Thirteen decisions are this file's, and each is written where it applies:
#
# - `Complex` is over `F64` only, where §5.10 writes `Complex of T`. The
#   generic record was tried and does not hold yet: there is no `Real`
#   interface to bound `T` by, so `Complex[T: Add + Sub + Mul + Div + Copy]`
#   admits `I64`, and this file's `0.0` and `1.0` would be accepted into an
#   `I64` slot unchecked — a type parameter is `assign`'s rule 1, which admits
#   anything. A field read through a shared `self` is also `&T` and is not
#   copied out for a `T: Copy` parameter, so every method would have to take
#   `self: Self`. `Complex[T: Real]` is the shape the day `Real` exists, and
#   it is a source change for every program that writes `Complex` bare.
# - The parts are the fields `re` and `im`, the names the task that commissioned
#   this file used and the ones every C, Fortran and NumPy reader already
#   knows. §5.10's `real` and `imaginary` are method names over the same parts,
#   not written yet; a field and a method are different things here (`x.re` is
#   a read, `x.re()` would be a call), so adding them later breaks nothing.
# - The method names are the notes': `conjugate` and `modulus_squared`, not
#   `conj` and `norm_sqr`.
# - A real factor is `scaled(by)`, not `z * 2.0`. An operator interface is
#   looked up by its method name and one implementation answers it, so a
#   second `Complex implements Mul:` taking an `F64` makes *both* `z * w` and
#   `z * 2.0` a refusal, and `2.0 * z` dispatches on `F64`, which is the
#   prelude's. `Mul[Rhs]` is the change that would allow it.
# - Division is Smith's algorithm (1962), not the textbook
#   `(a·c + b·d) / (c² + d²)`: the textbook squares the divisor's parts, so it
#   overflows to `inf` or underflows to `0` for any divisor with a part beyond
#   about `1e154` or below `1e-154` even when the quotient is an ordinary
#   number. Smith divides by the larger part first and never squares. A zero
#   divisor gives `NaN+NaNi`, where C99 Annex G's infinity recovery would give
#   an infinity; that recovery is not written.
# - `is` is IEEE equality part by part: `NaN` is not equal to itself and `0.0`
#   is equal to `-0.0`. It is the comparison `F64`'s own `is` makes, twice.
# - The layout is `{re: F64, im: F64}` and the record implements `ffi.CLayout`,
#   which is the layout of C's `double _Complex` and of LAPACKE's
#   `lapack_complex_double` under `LAPACK_COMPLEX_STRUCTURE`
#   (`c-binding-coverage.md` §2.3). So a `ffi.Span[Complex]` or a `&mut
#   Complex` can be handed to a `cblas_zdotc_sub` directly, and `ffi.Complex64`
#   stays what it is: the name a generated binding writes, declared bare in
#   the prelude. Making `ffi.Complex64` this type is the prelude naming a
#   module's type, which it cannot do; until it can, the two have one layout
#   and two names.
# - Every multivalued function takes its principal value, with the branch
#   cuts of C99 Annex G (`intrinsics-math-physics.md` §5.7 names
#   `argument_principal` and `ln_branch` and fixes no other convention). The
#   argument is `im.atan2(re)`, in `[-π, π]`; the cut is the negative real
#   axis, and the *sign of a zero imaginary part* says which side of it a
#   point is on: `ln(-1+0i)` is `0+πi` and `ln(-1-0i)` is `0-πi`, and `sqrt`
#   and `pow` inherit the same cut through `ln` and `argument`. `ln_branch`
#   and `argument_principal` are not written; `argument` is principal.
# - `sqrt` is the halved-parts form of `sqrt((|z| + |re|) / 2)`: both parts
#   are halved before `hypot`, so no finite input overflows, and the second
#   part is `im / 2t` (or `t` itself, with `im`'s sign, left of the imaginary
#   axis), so nothing cancels. `sqrt(x ± i∞)` is `+∞ ± i∞` and `sqrt(±0 ±
#   0i)` is `+0 ± 0i`, Annex G's two special cases that the formula alone
#   would get as `NaN`.
# - `pow(F64)` is polar, `|z|^x · (cos xθ + i sin xθ)`, and `pow_complex` is
#   `exp(w · ln z)`. Neither special-cases an integral exponent the way
#   CPython's `**` does, so `(1+1i).pow(2.0)` is `2i` to within an ulp, not
#   exactly; `z * z` is the exact square. A zero base gives `0+0i` for an
#   exponent with a positive real part and `1+0i` for an exponent of zero.
# - `ln` is `ln |z| + i·arg z`, with `|z|` from `hypot`, so it neither
#   overflows nor underflows; with no `ln_1p` it loses relative accuracy in
#   the real part for `|z|` within a few ulps of 1.
# - `tan` and `tanh` use the double-angle form, `tan(a+bi) = (sin 2a + i
#   sinh 2b) / (cos 2a + cosh 2b)`, which the quotient `sin z / cos z` is
#   not: that one overflows to `NaN` for `|b|` past about 355. Past `|b| =
#   20` the denominator is `cosh 2b` to every bit, and the parts are taken
#   as `2 sin 2a · e^(-2|b|)` and `tanh 2b`, which stay finite all the way
#   out.
# - `exp` keeps a zero imaginary part exactly: `exp(x + 0i)` is `e^x + 0i`,
#   where the formula would give `e^x · sin 0`, which is `NaN` at `x = ∞`.

# --- Complex ------------------------------------------------------------------

# A complex number `re + im·i`, two `F64`s in that order.
public type Complex:
    re: F64
    im: F64

Complex implements ffi.CLayout

Complex implements Copy

Complex has:
    # `re + im·i`. The record literal `Complex(re: 1.0, im: 2.0)` is the
    # same value, with the names written out.
    public def new(re: F64, im: F64) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

    # A real number on the complex plane: `re + 0i`.
    public def from_real(re: F64) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: re, im: 0.0)

    # `re - im·i`, the reflection across the real axis.
    public def conjugate(self) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: self.re, im: -self.im)

    # `re² + im²`: the squared modulus, which needs no square root. It
    # overflows where the modulus would not, for a part beyond about `1e154`.
    public def modulus_squared(self) -> F64:
        self.re * self.re + self.im * self.im

    # Both parts multiplied by a real `factor` — the `z * 2.0` the operator
    # cannot be (see the header).
    public def scaled(self, by: F64) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: self.re * by, im: self.im * by)

# Whether `x` carries a minus sign, `-0.0` included: `-0.0 < 0.0` is false, and
# `1.0 / -0.0` is `-inf`. `NaN` answers false.
def is_negative(x: F64) -> Bool:
    x < 0.0 or (x is 0.0 and 1.0 / x < 0.0)

# `magnitude` with the sign of `sign`, zeros included: C's `copysign`, which
# `F64` does not have.
def with_sign_of(magnitude: F64, sign: F64) -> F64:
    if is_negative(sign):
        return -magnitude.abs()
    magnitude.abs()

# --- Polar form ---------------------------------------------------------------

Complex has:
    # The point at distance `modulus` from the origin, at angle `argument`
    # radians from the positive real axis.
    public def from_polar(modulus: F64, argument: F64) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: modulus * argument.cos(), im: modulus * argument.sin())

    # `|z|`, through `hypot`: no overflow where `modulus_squared` would.
    public def modulus(self) -> F64:
        self.re.hypot(self.im)

    # The principal argument, `im.atan2(re)`, in `[-π, π]`. The sign of a
    # zero imaginary part picks the side of the cut (see the header).
    public def argument(self) -> F64:
        self.im.atan2(self.re)

    # `(modulus, argument)`.
    public def to_polar(self) -> (F64, F64):
        (self.modulus(), self.argument())

# --- Exponential, logarithm, roots and powers -------------------------------

Complex has:
    # `e^re · (cos im + i sin im)`, with a zero `im` kept as it is.
    public def exp(self) -> Complex:
        let scale be self.re.exp()
        if self.im is 0.0:
            return Complex(re: scale, im: self.im)
        Complex(re: scale * self.im.cos(), im: scale * self.im.sin())

    # The principal logarithm, `ln |z| + i·arg z`. `ln(0)` is `-∞` plus the
    # zero's argument.
    public def ln(self) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: self.modulus().ln(), im: self.argument())

    # The principal square root: a non-negative real part, and an imaginary
    # part with the sign of `im` (see the header for the formulation).
    public def sqrt(self) -> Complex:
        if self.im.is_infinite():
            return Complex(re: F64.INFINITY, im: self.im)
        if self.re is 0.0 and self.im is 0.0:
            return Complex(re: 0.0, im: self.im)
        let half_modulus be (self.re * 0.5).hypot(self.im * 0.5)
        let t be (self.re.abs() * 0.5 + half_modulus).sqrt()
        if self.re >= 0.0:
            return Complex(re: t, im: self.im / (2.0 * t))
        Complex(re: self.im.abs() / (2.0 * t), im: with_sign_of(t, self.im))

    # `z^x` for a real exponent, in polar form on the principal branch.
    public def pow(self, exponent: F64) -> Complex:
        if exponent is 0.0:
            return Complex(re: 1.0, im: 0.0)
        if self.re is 0.0 and self.im is 0.0 and exponent > 0.0:
            return Complex(re: 0.0, im: 0.0)
        let modulus be self.modulus().pow(exponent)
        Complex.from_polar(modulus, self.argument() * exponent)

    # `z^w`, as `exp(w · ln z)` on the principal branch.
    public def pow_complex(self, exponent: Complex) -> Complex:
        if exponent.re is 0.0 and exponent.im is 0.0:
            return Complex(re: 1.0, im: 0.0)
        if self.re is 0.0 and self.im is 0.0 and exponent.re > 0.0:
            return Complex(re: 0.0, im: 0.0)
        (exponent * self.ln()).exp()

# --- Trigonometric and hyperbolic ---------------------------------------------

Complex has:
    # `sin a cosh b + i cos a sinh b`.
    public def sin(self) -> Complex:
        let re be self.re.sin() * self.im.cosh()
        let im be self.re.cos() * self.im.sinh()
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

    # `cos a cosh b - i sin a sinh b`.
    public def cos(self) -> Complex:
        let re be self.re.cos() * self.im.cosh()
        let im be -self.re.sin() * self.im.sinh()
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

    # The double-angle form, with the cutover at `|b| = 20` (see the header).
    public def tan(self) -> Complex:
        let a be 2.0 * self.re
        let b be 2.0 * self.im
        if self.im.abs() > 20.0:
            let re be 2.0 * a.sin() * (-b.abs()).exp()
            return Complex(re: re, im: b.tanh())
        let denominator be a.cos() + b.cosh()
        Complex(re: a.sin() / denominator, im: b.sinh() / denominator)

    # `sinh a cos b + i cosh a sin b`.
    public def sinh(self) -> Complex:
        let re be self.re.sinh() * self.im.cos()
        let im be self.re.cosh() * self.im.sin()
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

    # `cosh a cos b + i sinh a sin b`.
    public def cosh(self) -> Complex:
        let re be self.re.cosh() * self.im.cos()
        let im be self.re.sinh() * self.im.sin()
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

    # `tan`'s form with the parts' roles exchanged: `tanh(a+bi) = (sinh 2a +
    # i sin 2b) / (cosh 2a + cos 2b)`, and `tanh 2a` past `|a| = 20`.
    public def tanh(self) -> Complex:
        let a be 2.0 * self.re
        let b be 2.0 * self.im
        if self.re.abs() > 20.0:
            let im be 2.0 * b.sin() * (-a.abs()).exp()
            return Complex(re: a.tanh(), im: im)
        let denominator be a.cosh() + b.cos()
        Complex(re: a.sinh() / denominator, im: b.sin() / denominator)

# --- Arithmetic ---------------------------------------------------------------

Complex implements Add:
    def add(self, other: Complex) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: self.re + other.re, im: self.im + other.im)

Complex implements Sub:
    def sub(self, other: Complex) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: self.re - other.re, im: self.im - other.im)

# `(a + bi)(c + di) = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i`, four products and no fused
# multiply-add, so it rounds as the formula reads.
Complex implements Mul:
    def mul(self, other: Complex) -> Complex:
        let re be self.re * other.re - self.im * other.im
        let im be self.re * other.im + self.im * other.re
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

# Smith's algorithm (see the header): divide through by the divisor's larger
# part, so that `ratio` is at most 1 in magnitude and nothing is squared.
Complex implements Div:
    def div(self, other: Complex) -> Complex:
        if other.re.abs() >= other.im.abs():
            let ratio be other.im / other.re
            let denominator be other.re + other.im * ratio
            let re be (self.re + self.im * ratio) / denominator
            let im be (self.im - self.re * ratio) / denominator
            return Complex(re: re, im: im)
        let ratio be other.re / other.im
        let denominator be other.re * ratio + other.im
        let re be (self.re * ratio + self.im) / denominator
        let im be (self.im * ratio - self.re) / denominator
        Complex(re: re, im: im)

Complex implements Neg:
    def neg(self) -> Complex:
        Complex(re: -self.re, im: -self.im)

Complex implements Eq:
    def eq(self, other: Complex) -> Bool:
        self.re is other.re and self.im is other.im

# --- Display ------------------------------------------------------------------

# `1.5+2.0i`, `1.5-2.0i`, `0.0-0.0i`: each part as `print` writes an `F64`,
# with no spaces and the sign of the imaginary part always written, so a
# negative one reads `a-bi` rather than `a+-bi`. Both parts go through
# `number`, so both will render under the caller's spec once `f"{z:.2}"` is
# built (`SC0173` refuses a spec today).
Complex implements Display:
    def display(self, into: &mut Formatter):
        into.number(self.re)
        if is_negative(self.im):
            into.raw("-")
            into.number(-self.im)
        else:
            into.raw("+")
            into.number(self.im)
        into.raw("i")
